/-! ## Part XII — Sharp no-side squeeze at three exact-ratio anchors

This part removes the last blocker from Part XI. The threshold there proved only
the never-exceeds direction: for every cutoff ratio strictly below the arm's
geometric critical point, the budget head is never attained on the no-side. The
complement — that at the exact critical ratio the head *is* eventually attained —
required squeezing a prefix sum of the response trajectory over 100+ coupling
windows. The monolithic encoding of that chain overflowed the kernel's memory
budget at compile time, and bisections bounded the failure below width 60 and
above width 120.

### The construction

The three squeeze theorems below (`noSideForcedSharp1..3`, one per arm, plus
their existential forms `existsSharp1..3`) establish the sharp direction with a
*sliding-window* encoding. Each theorem carries exactly one window of history
between adjacent steps:

- a **source-floor chain** `l_n : chat n ≥ L_n`, where `L` is the ceiling-iterate
  of the arm's ratio a/b started at 39000;
- a **response potential chain** `q_n : 1000^n · dhat n ≥ XB_n`, the exact
  1000:1-damped potential inherited from the launch cap `dhat 0 ≥ 1000000`
  combined with the source floors;
- a **cumulative prefix chain**
  `p_n : 999 · 1000^(n-1) · sumTo dhat n ≥ CUM_n`, the closed-form weighted
  identity
  `999 · 1000^(K-1) · Σ_{i<K} dhat i = (1000^K − 1) · dhat 0 + Σ_j chat j · (1000^K − 1000^(j+1))`
  evaluated against the floors;
- **consumed chain links are cleared per window**, so every `omega` call sees a
  bounded slice of the constraint graph while the arithmetic remains exact
  end-to-end. All coefficients in the certificate are nonnegative, so no upper
  bound on any quantity is ever needed.

The comparison principle `compareMono` (already machine-checked, at the top of
this file) lifts each minimal-trajectory certificate to ordinally larger
admissible trajectories.

